  <h1>THE ONE IDEA, PHRASED AS A CLAIM WITH A VERB IN IT</h1>
  <p class="paper-meta">
    <span class="pill med">working</span>
    <span class="pill">10 min</span>
    ONE LINE OF FRAMING, OR THE ATTRIBUTION IF THIS PAGE FOLLOWS A SOURCE.
    &middot; <a href="https://example.com/primary-source">SOURCE</a>
  </p>

  <div class="card tldr">
    <h2 class="h-label">The one-minute version</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>The problem that exists before this idea, and why the obvious fix does not work.</li>
      <li>The idea itself, with <b>its name in bold</b> so the reader can search for it later.</li>
      <li>The mechanism in one sentence.</li>
      <li>What it costs, because a page that lists only benefits is unfinished.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2>1. Why this exists</h2>
  <p>Open on the problem, in the reader's own terms, before naming anything new. The first diagram belongs here: a picture of the situation before the idea arrives, so the idea has somewhere to land.</p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <div class="mermaid">
flowchart LR
  A["Before: the obvious approach"] --> B["Where it breaks"]
    </div>
    <figcaption>Plain English reading of the figure, ending with <b>the one thing to carry away</b>.</figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>2. The mental model</h2>
  <p>The picture the reader should hold. One analogy, carried consistently for the rest of the page. Name every new term in plain words the first time it appears here, not once per course.</p>

  <figure class="diagram">
    <div class="mermaid">
sequenceDiagram
  participant A as Caller
  participant B as Service
  A->>B: request
  Note over B: A label that needs two lines&lt;br/&gt;writes the break as an entity
  B-->>A: response
    </div>
    <figcaption>Pick the diagram kind by what the reader is confused about, not by habit. <b>A second kind on the page is a floor, not a flourish.</b></figcaption>
  </figure>

  <h2>3. The mechanism</h2>
  <p>Now how it actually works. Plain English before any formula, and the formula only once the reader can picture what it measures.</p>

  <div class="math">
    FORMULA
    <span class="gloss">Read as: NAME EVERY SYMBOL IN WORDS. No formula ships without this.</span>
  </div>

  <ol class="worked">
    <li><b>The first step.</b> Work the arithmetic the source compressed, so the reader never reconstructs a missing step.</li>
    <li><b>The answer.</b> A figure quoted from a source is marked <span class="keynum">1,225</span>; a figure derived here shows its arithmetic.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>4. What it costs</h2>
  <p>The trade-off, named in the same section as the technique that incurs it, and the failure mode when it fails.</p>

  <div class="callout warn">
    The failure that is silent, expensive, or both. Also where an honest limit goes: a source that is loose, or an assumption doing more work than the evidence supports. At most one per page.
  </div>

